# `engines` in package.json is a refusal, not a suggestion.
#
# npm's default is to warn — `npm warn EBADENGINE` scrolls past in an install
# that then succeeds — and the version it warned about goes on to fail somewhere
# with no memory of the warning. Measured on Node 20.19.5, which #269 dropped
# from the range: `npm test` ran for **360 seconds** and ended in `Worker exited
# unexpectedly` forty-three times, no tests run, nothing anywhere naming the Node
# version or the option the worker choked on. With this line the same checkout
# refuses in three seconds, naming the required range and the version in hand.
#
# It is the same idea as the tracked `doc.go` in backend/internal/core/generated:
# fail in the place that can say why, rather than at the first thing downstream
# that happens to notice. Verified not to fire on anything in range — every
# dependency's own `engines` is satisfied on 22, 24 and 26.
engine-strict=true
